{
  "id": "80877",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Effect of Change in Pressure on Equilibrium",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Sometimes we can change the position of equilibrium by changing the pressure of a system. However, changes in pressure have a measurable effect "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "only in systems in which gases are involved"
              },
              {
                "text": "—and then, only when the chemical reaction produces a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "change"
              },
              {
                "text": " in the total number of gas molecules in the system. An easy way to recognize such a system is to look for different numbers of moles of gas on the reactant and product sides of the equilibrium. While evaluating pressure (as well as related factors like volume), it is important to remember that equilibrium constants are defined with regard to concentration (for "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "c"
              },
              {
                "text": ") or partial pressure (for "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "p"
              },
              {
                "text": "). Some changes to pressure (or volume), such as adding an inert gas that is not part of the equilibrium, will change the total pressure but not perturb the equilibrium. If an inert gas is added without changing the volume, the total pressure increases but the partial pressures of the gases in the system are constant, and the equilibrium does not shift. If the volume changes due to the addition of an inert gas, the partial pressures and concentrations of the gases also change, and the system will shift in the direction that relieves the stress caused by the change."
              }
            ],
            "id": "5v7b5b5ze3h398h",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If we decrease the volume of a gaseous system at equilibrium, the partial pressures of the gases will increase. In accordance with Le Châtelier's principle, the system will adjust to reduce the increased pressure. Because pressure is directly proportional to moles of gas, a shift in the direction that decreases the number of molecules per unit of volume will be favored because it relieves the stress."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4qarillwjs1iydu",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Consider what happens when we decrease the volume—thereby increasing the pressure—of a system in which NO, O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " are at equilibrium:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3lj0ypxajgbpew1",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "6wrsgp2avphw6kb",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "124qgz6yubjjk0o",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;\\mathrm{NO}(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;2\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_2(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The formation of additional amounts of NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " decreases the total number of molecules in the system because each time two molecules of NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " form, a total of three molecules of NO and O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " are consumed. This reduces the total pressure exerted by the system and reduces, but does not completely relieve, the stress of the increased pressure. On the other hand, a decrease in the pressure on the system favors "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "decomposition"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A chemical reaction in which one reactant breaks apart down into two or more products."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "200453989",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2134896508",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of NO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " into NO and O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", which tends to restore the pressure."
              }
            ],
            "id": "zuji2m83098lzn3",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Now consider this reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ywsmo7z5aa9kpiz",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "73prsbvh3p1ug61",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "pjvobfioid7z8lo",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm N}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{NO}(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Because there is no change in the total number of molecules in the system during reaction, a change in pressure does not favor either formation or decomposition of gaseous nitrogen monoxide."
              }
            ],
            "id": "w4kk60bbeyy0480",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "810135214",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
